<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuru kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200–500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kuru kingdom was a Vedic Indo-Aryan realm of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, centred on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> region around present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in roughly 1200–500 BCE. It played a major part in shaping Vedic ritual and the collection of the Vedic texts. The Kurus later counted among the sixteen mahajanapadas, and their dynastic war forms the setting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kuru kingdom" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roshan Krishna Dhanawade (Srimant ROSHAN) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Kuru_Kingdom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Khalimpur plate, the kings of the Bhojas, Matsyas, Madras, <b>Kurus</b>, Yadus, Yavanas, Avanti, Gandhara and Kira attended the ceremony and approved it, a claim that at least shows the scale of Dharmapala’s ambitions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Kuru kingdom</b>, with which it is associated, systematised the Vedic religious literature and developed the Śrauta ritual of sacrifice.</blockquote><a href="/culture/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In the later period the <b>Kuru kingdom</b> systematised the religious literature and developed the Śrauta ritual.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mahabharata's setting has a historical precedent in Iron Age (Vedic) India, where the <b>Kuru kingdom</b> was the centre of political power during roughly 1200 to 800 BCE, and some elements of the epic can be traced back to Vedic times.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>With the later kingdoms, among them the <b>Kuru</b>, hereditary kingship emerged and the councils declined.</blockquote><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Matsyas appear in the Rigveda and later Vedic texts as a people west of the Yamuna and south of the <b>Kurus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/matsya">Matsya</a></li></ul></section>
